    "document_metadata": {
      "title": "A minimal PBPK model for the fictional drug Fiktivol in renal impairment",
      "authors": [
        "Erika Mustermann, Beispiel-Universität, Musterstadt",
        "Jane Doe, Example Institute of Pharmacometrics"
      ],
      "abstract": "Fiktivol is a fictional compound that is cleared mainly by the renal route. We use it to illustrate a physiologically based pharmacokinetic (PBPK) workflow from model building to dose recommendation. A minimal PBPK model with a liver compartment and glomerular filtration was calibrated against simulated plasma concentrations. In virtual patients with severe renal impairment the model predicts a 2.4-fold higher area under the curve, which supports halving the maintenance dose."
    }
  },
  "content": {
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "section_1",
        "title": "1 Introduction",
        "type": "section",
        "level": 1,
        "elements": [
          {
            "id": "section_1_elem_1",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "coordinates": null,
            "text": "Renal impairment changes the exposure of drugs that are eliminated by glomerular filtration or tubular secretion. Regulatory guidance therefore asks for dedicated studies or model-based predictions before a drug is given to patients with reduced renal function.",
            "statistics": {
              "words": 37,
              "characters": 225,
              "sentences": 2
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "section_1_elem_2",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "coordinates": null,
            "text": "PBPK models describe absorption, distribution and elimination with physiological parameters such as organ volumes, blood flows and the glomerular filtration rate. Because these parameters change with disease, a model qualified in healthy volunteers can be extrapolated to patients with impaired renal function.",
            "statistics": {
              "words": 42,
              "characters": 269,
              "sentences": 2
            }
          }
        ],
        "statistics": {
          "word_count": 79,
          "character_count": 494,
          "sentence_count": 4,
          "element_count": 2,
          "paragraph_count": 2,
          "list_count": 0
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "section_2",
        "title": "2 Methods",
        "type": "section",
        "level": 1,
        "elements": [
          {
            "id": "section_2_elem_1",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "coordinates": null,
            "text": "All data in this example are simulated. The model was implemented as a system of ordinary differential equations.",
            "statistics": {
              "words": 18,
              "characters": 96,
              "sentences": 2
            }
          }
        ],
        "statistics": {
          "word_count": 18,
          "character_count": 96,
          "sentence_count": 2,
          "element_count": 1,
          "paragraph_count": 1,
          "list_count": 0
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "section_3",
        "title": "2.1 Model structure",
        "type": "section",
        "level": 1,
        "elements": [
          {
            "id": "section_3_elem_1",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "coordinates": null,
            "text": "The model consists of a central plasma compartment, a liver compartment with hepatic blood flow and a lumped compartment for the remaining tissues. Renal clearance is the product of the unbound fraction and the glomerular filtration rate, plus a small secretion term. Hepatic metabolism accounts for about ten percent of total clearance in healthy volunteers.",
            "statistics": {
              "words": 55,
              "characters": 305,
              "sentences": 3
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "section_3_elem_2",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "coordinates": null,
            "text": "For each group 500 virtual patients were sampled with body weight and age drawn from fictional distributions.",
            "statistics": {
              "words": 17,
              "characters": 93,
              "sentences": 1
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "section_3_elem_3",
            "type": "list",
            "coordinates": null,
            "items": [
              "Healthy renal function: glomerular filtration rate 100 mL/min",
              "Moderate renal impairment: 45 mL/min",
              "Severe renal impairment: 20 mL/min"
            ],
            "item_count": 3,
            "statistics": {
              "words": 18,
              "characters": 116,
              "sentences": 1
            }
          }
        ],
        "statistics": {
          "word_count": 90,
          "character_count": 514,
          "sentence_count": 4,
          "element_count": 3,
          "paragraph_count": 2,
          "list_count": 1
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "section_4",
        "title": "3 Results",
        "type": "section",
        "level": 1,
        "elements": [
          {
            "id": "section_4_elem_1",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "coordinates": null,
            "text": "In healthy virtual patients the simulated plasma concentrations of Fiktivol peaked about two hours after an oral dose of 100 mg and declined with a terminal half-life of nine hours. The predicted area under the concentration-time curve matched the simulated reference data within ten percent, and the visual predictive check showed no systematic deviation over the dosing interval. Sensitivity analysis identified the glomerular filtration rate and the unbound fraction as the parameters with the largest influence on exposure, whereas hepatic blood flow had almost no effect. These results indicate that the minimal model captures the main elimination pathway of the compound and can be used for extrapolation to other populations.",
            "statistics": {
              "words": 110,
              "characters": 623,
              "sentences": 4
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "section_4_elem_2",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "coordinates": null,
            "text": "In moderate renal impairment the model predicts a 1.6-fold increase in the area under the curve and a prolonged half-life of fourteen hours. In severe renal impairment the increase reaches 2.4-fold, with a half-life of twenty-one hours, while the peak concentration rises by only thirty percent because absorption is unchanged. The between-patient variability of exposure grows with decreasing filtration rate, so that the upper tail of the distribution in severe impairment exceeds the exposure range that was studied in healthy volunteers. Accumulation after repeated dosing is therefore the main safety concern, and the time to reach steady state increases from about two days to almost five days in the severe group.",
            "statistics": {
              "words": 111,
              "characters": 610,
              "sentences": 6
            }
          },
          {
            "id": "section_4_elem_3",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "coordinates": null,
            "text": "Dose adjustment was explored by simulating maintenance doses of 100, 75 and 50 mg once daily. Halving the dose in severe renal impairment brought the steady-state exposure back into the range observed in healthy volunteers, whereas a reduction to 75 mg was sufficient in moderate impairment. A loading dose was not required because the therapeutic effect of Fiktivol is assumed to depend on average exposure rather than on early peak concentrations. The recommended regimen was robust to a twenty percent misspecification of the unbound fraction, which we varied in a separate scenario analysis. All simulations were repeated with ten different random seeds and gave the same dose recommendation for every group.",
            "statistics": {
              "words": 111,
              "characters": 602,
              "sentences": 5
            }
          }
        ],
        "statistics": {
          "word_count": 332,
          "character_count": 1835,
          "sentence_count": 15,
          "element_count": 3,
          "paragraph_count": 3,
          "list_count": 0
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "section_5",
        "title": "4 Discussion",
        "type": "section",
        "level": 1,
        "elements": [
          {
            "id": "section_5_elem_1",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "coordinates": null,
            "text": "The example shows how a PBPK model that is qualified in healthy volunteers can support dosing decisions in renal impairment when clinical data are scarce. The main limitation is that all inputs are fictional; in a real application the glomerular filtration rate distribution, the unbound fraction in uraemic plasma and possible changes in transporter activity would have to be informed by data. A second limitation is the lumped tissue compartment, which cannot describe drugs with strong tissue binding.",
            "statistics": {
              "words": 78,
              "characters": 427,
              "sentences": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "statistics": {
          "word_count": 78,
          "character_count": 427,
          "sentence_count": 3,
          "element_count": 1,
          "paragraph_count": 1,
          "list_count": 0
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "section_6",
        "title": "5 Conclusion",
        "type": "section",
        "level": 1,
